WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.200
“AAHAHH!!!”

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.440
Two questions.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.840
Number one. Why is Mel Gibson fighting a beaver puppet?

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.280
Two. How did this movie get made?

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.560
The Beaver is a 2011 dark comedy about depression.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:31.000
It is so pitch black dark it’s basically
a drama — with a talking beaver puppet.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.880
The movie was not a hit.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:39.469
It was a failed comeback attempt for Mel Gibson after anti-semitic tirades, DUIs, and allegations

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:40.469
of abuse.

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:45.710
But you can’t forget that this movie is
deeply, unflinchingly, weird.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:53.080
It got made at a time when the top ten movies at the box office were all parts of larger franchises.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.160
Jacob: "You guys...

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.200
...really look great together."

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.280
How did this happen?

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.590
“Allow me to briefly explain.”

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.139
If you go to screenwriter Kyle Killen’s old

00:01:09.139 --> 00:01:11.909
Blogspot blog, there’s a clue.

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:16.319
A post from December 11th, 2008 called
“Blacklisted.”

00:01:16.319 --> 00:01:20.279
That’s because in 2008, Kyle Killen landed on

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.109
the top of “The Blacklist.”

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:29.259
Since 2005, a survey called The Black List
has recorded the most liked, unproduced screenplays

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:31.079
bouncing around Hollywood.

00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:36.009
Industry insiders submit their favorite unproduced screenplays, and then the anonymous votes

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:37.909
are tallied and published.

00:01:37.909 --> 00:01:39.869
It’s that simple.

00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:42.719
But it’s quickly become a huge source of
buzz.

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:46.119
And it’s based on scripts alone.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.520
And by looking at The Blacklist's success,
you can learn something about how all these

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:58.520
movies — from number one to number 216 — actually get made.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.920
When Franklin Leonard started his black list survey, he didn’t have a master plan.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:09.920
Just a big idea.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.269
Franklin: You have a much greater chance of making a great movie with a great script, than you do if you don’t have one.”

00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:18.110
So he started something in December 2005.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:25.080
“The Black Llist started as a...survey.
My job was I was working for Leonardo DiCaprio’s film production company."

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.349
"My job was to find great scripts."

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.400
"I felt like I was doing a very bad job at my job. I wanted to do a better job at my job."

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.959
“I sent an email to 75 of my peers and said send me a list of your 10 favorite screenplays

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:38.210
that haven’t yet been produced, and in exchange I will send you the combined list.”

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:38.920
"They all voted."

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.200
"And I put it all on an Excel spreadsheet."

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.120
"Ran a pivot table"

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:42.629
"Output it to PowerPoint."

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:45.340
"Put a quasi subversive name on it: 'The
Black List,'

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.360
was a reference to both the Hollywood blacklist of the McCarthy era."

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.360
"And sent it out.”

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.880
"I think that the The Black List only could have come into existence at the time it did."

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:05.080
The first Black List went viral the same
weekend that Lazy Sunday went viral online — from Saturday Night Live."

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:11.120
“The way in which we consume information online and the way in which we share information online,

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.120
that was, I think, the beginning of that moment where all those relationships and

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:19.280
all of that that we were comfortable with in terms of sharing information, for better or for worse, started changing.”

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.800
Suddenly, a list of great stories could show up in one place and be distributed around

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.280
the world, thanks to the internet everywhere.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.569
That 2005 Black List included future hit movies like Juno.

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:36.960
But to understand its real influence, you
have to go beyond the list.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.160
Let’s say getting a movie made requires a certain amount of buzz.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.840
Let's make the metaphor really literal here.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.440
Take 2011’s top movie, Harry Potter And
the Deathly Hallows, Part II.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:55.400
It’s a franchise, last one was a hit, it
has huge stars — there’s no question about buzz.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.280
It gets made.

00:03:58.299 --> 00:04:01.310
Now imagine a movie like The Beaver.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:08.090
“I think it's important to remember that, every year, there are something like 50,000 screenplays registered at the Writers Guild. Every year."

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:14.320
"If you are a particularly industrious reader, maybe you’ll read a thousand screenplays a year.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:22.650
So, obviously, there’s a gap of some 49,000 scripts. Just in that single year that are submitted to Hollywood generally— that you're not going to read.”

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.020
Now The Beaver was not plucked from nowhere.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.449
Kyle Killen had representation, and it had been picked up by a production company.

00:04:30.449 --> 00:04:34.669
Steve Carell was even attached before the
2008 Black List came out.

00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:38.050
But this movie was not Harry Potter.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:40.139
Winning the Black List made the buzz public

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:47.259
and, at the same time, sustained that buzz through actor and director changes.

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:54.509
It kept going until a movie about a talking
beaver puppet improbably, miraculously, actually got made.

00:04:55.509 --> 00:05:00.060
“We just say: send us a list of your favorite screenplays that haven’t yet been produced.”

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.880
“You end up with a list that includes everyone from, Aaron Sorkin and David Benioff,

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:11.400
and people who literally  this is their first screenplay and they've just arrived in  Hollywood or they're still living outside of LA.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.040
Kyle Killen was not yet a Hollywood insider.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.120
But the Black List clearly helped his movie
keep buzzing.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.800
Dissecting hype is hard.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.800
Take 2012’s Argo.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.919
It got 28 votes on the 2010 Black List.

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:27.389
It already had a reputation.

00:05:27.389 --> 00:05:33.080
It was based on a great 2007 Joshua Bearman Wired article, optioned by George Clooney.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:37.470
They got former CIA agent Tony Mendez involved.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.110
So it had energy.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.000
But the buzz kind of leveled off.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.150
Then after the 2010 Black List came out, Ben Affleck decided to direct.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:52.599
The Blacklist didn’t make Argo or Juno happen, but it got the scripts noticed.

00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:57.230
It didn’t singlehandedly greenlight the
hundreds of movies it’s featured.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:58.860
Some you’ve heard of.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:05:59.860
Some you haven’t.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:05.470
But it has amplified buzz that isn’t about big stars in amusement park ride adaptations,

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.690
toy friendly sequels, or superhero franchises.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:12.220
It’s buzz about words.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:13.920
Stories.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.440
Today the Black List is a well-oiled machine,

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:24.480
including its own podcasts, events, and online submission network.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:28.640
And it’s all focused on generating hype
about...writing.

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:37.900
“I know when I am reading a good script
because the rest of the world ceases to exist...and

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:42.180
and when you’re finished, you’re a little
sad because you don’t get to spend more

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.210
time with those characters.”

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:47.090
The Beaver was not a box office hit.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:51.090
It ended up in 216th place in 2011.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.680
The Rotten Tomatoes rating?

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:53.680
61%.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.800
Fine, but not great.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:02.590
In the beginning of The Beaver, there's a scene where Mel Gibson is about to walk back to his car...

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:04.610
and drive home.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:05.600
But then he stops.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.200
Mel sees something mangy and weird in the pile of trash.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.400
He pulls The Beaver out.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:17.480
And then?

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.000
Everything changes.

